# For every single commercial computer you have ever 
# seen or used (this inlcudes PCs, cellphones, smart watches
# calculators, laptops, Chromebooks . . . all of them!),
# everything is does has to me some form of function.
# That is to say computers don't deal with ambiguity.
# If needs clear instructions that will lead to clear 
# outputs.
#
# Imagine, then, how can computers deal with randomness?
#
# The answer is simple: It Can't!
#
# Instead, computers (and their users) must settle for
# pseudo-random things. psuedo-random generators perform
# some convoluted function that seems random but their is 
# definitely some algorithm that creates the pseudo-random
# values. 
#
# Typically, random functions take a seed value which is run
# through the random function to create the pseudo-random 
# number. Then, the next time the function is run, the 
# previous output is used as the new input value. This process
# continues until the seed value is manually reset or the 
# platform's memory is reset.

# Typically the seed value comes from looking inside the 
# machine motherboard and reading the current data and
# time and and converting this into a suitable number. This 
# ensures that everytime you run a program there is a unique
# seed and thus a unique chain of values that seem fairly
# random.
#

# In pyret, we can generate random integers with the 
# following function:

# num-random :: (max :: Number) -> Number
# Returns a pseudo-random integer from 0 to max - 1.

# For example:
"each time you run these four expressions, you get something different:"
num-random( 4)     # will generate a pseudo-random number from 0 to 3 
num-random( 15)    # will generate a pseudo-random number from 0 to 15
num-random( 6) + 1 # will generate a pseudo-random number from 1 to 6
num-random(20) + 5 # will generate a pseudo-random number from 5 to 25





